lled his family in car","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eArticle URL: \u003ca href=\"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c70n2x7p22do\"\u003ehttps://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c70n2x7p22do\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47402950\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47402950\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 868\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 259\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c70n2x7p22do","external_url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47402950","date_published":"2026-03-16T18:38:14Z","author":{"name":"tartoran","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=tartoran"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47402651","title":"The return-to-the-office trend backfires","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eArticle URL: \u003ca href=\"https://thehill.com/opinion/technology/5775420-remote-first-productivity-growth/\"\u003ehttps://thehill.com/opinion/technology/5775420-remote-first-productivity-growth/\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47402651\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47402651\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 108\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 87\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://thehill.com/opinion/technology/5775420-remote-first-productivity-growth/","external_url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47402651","date_published":"2026-03-16T18:13:18Z","author":{"name":"penguin_booze","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=penguin_booze"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47402640","title":"Meta’s renewed commitment to jemalloc","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003e\u003ca href=\"https://github.com/jemalloc/jemalloc\" rel=\"nofollow\"\u003ehttps://github.com/jemalloc/jemalloc\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003chr\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47402640\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47402640\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 311\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 128\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://engineering.fb.com/2026/03/02/data-infrastructure/investing-in-infrastructure-metas-renewed-commitment-to-jemalloc/","external_url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47402640","date_published":"2026-03-16T18:12:32Z","author":{"name":"hahahacorn","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=hahahacorn"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47401879","title":"The “small web” is bigger than you might think","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eArticle URL: \u003ca href=\"https://kevinboone.me/small_web_is_big.html\"\u003ehttps://kevinboone.me/small_web_is_big.html\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47401879\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47401879\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 284\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 127\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://kevinboone.me/small_web_is_big.html","external_url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47401879","date_published":"2026-03-16T17:17:57Z","author":{"name":"speckx","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=speckx"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47401809","title":"US commercial insurers pay 254% of Medicare for the same hospital procedures","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eArticle URL: \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/rexrodeo/american-healthcare-conundrum\"\u003ehttps://github.com/rexrodeo/american-healthcare-conundrum\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47401809\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47401809\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 127\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 74\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://github.com/rexrodeo/american-healthcare-conundrum","external_url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47401809","date_published":"2026-03-16T17:13:27Z","author":{"name":"rexroad","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=rexroad"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47400868","title":"Show HN: Claude Code skills that build complete Godot games","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eI’ve been working on this for about a year through four major rewrites. Godogen is a pipeline that takes a text prompt, designs the architecture, generates 2D/3D assets, writes the GDScript, and tests it visually. The output is a complete, playable Godot 4 project.\u003cp\u003eGetting LLMs to reliably generate functional games required solving three specific engineering bottlenecks:\u003cp\u003e1. The Training Data Scarcity: LLMs barely know GDScript. It has ~850 classes and a Python-like syntax that will happily let a model hallucinate Python idioms that fail to compile. To fix this, I built a custom reference system: a hand-written language spec, full API docs converted from Godot's XML source, and a quirks database for engine behaviors you can't learn from docs alone. Because 850 classes blow up the context window, the agent lazy-loads only the specific APIs it needs at runtime.\u003cp\u003e2. The Build-Time vs. Runtime State: Scenes are generated by headless scripts that build the node graph in memory and serialize it to .tscn files. This avoids the fragility of hand-editing Godot's serialization format. But it means certain engine features (like `@onready` or signal connections) aren't available at build time—they only exist when the game actually runs. Teaching the model which APIs are available at which phase — and that every node needs its owner set correctly or it silently vanishes on save — took careful prompting but paid off.\u003cp\u003e3. The Evaluation Loop: A coding agent is inherently biased toward its own output. To stop it from cheating, a separate Gemini Flash agent acts as visual QA. It sees only the rendered screenshots from the running engine—no code—and compares them against a generated reference image. It catches the visual bugs text analysis misses: z-fighting, floating objects, physics explosions, and grid-like placements that should be organic.\u003cp\u003eArchitecturally, it runs as two Claude Code skills: an orchestrator that plans the pipeline, and a task executor that implements each piece in a `context: fork` window so mistakes and state don't accumulate.\u003cp\u003eEverything is open source: \u003ca href=\"https://github.com/htdt/godogen\" rel=\"nofollow\"\u003ehttps://github.com/htdt/godogen\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003eDemo video (real games, not cherry-picked screenshots): \u003ca href=\"https://youtu.be/eUz19GROIpY\" rel=\"nofollow\"\u003ehttps://youtu.be/eUz19GROIpY\u003c/a\u003e\u003cp\u003eBlog post with the full story (all the wrong turns) coming soon. Happy to answer questions.\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003chr\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47400868\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47400868\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 132\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 77\u003c/p\u003e\n","url":"https://github.com/htdt/godogen","external_url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47400868","date_published":"2026-03-16T16:07:29Z","author":{"name":"htdt","url":"https://news.ycombinator.com/user?id=htdt"}},{"id":"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47400261","title":"Apideck CLI – An AI-agent interface with much lower context consumption than MCP","content_html":"\n\u003cp\u003eArticle URL: \u003ca href=\"https://www.apideck.com/blog/mcp-server-eating-context-window-cli-alternative\"\u003ehttps://www.apideck.com/blog/mcp-server-eating-context-window-cli-alternative\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003eComments URL: \u003ca href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47400261\"\u003ehttps://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=47400261\u003c/a\u003e\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003ePoints: 113\u003c/p\u003e\n\u003cp\u003e# Comments: 103\u003c/p\u003e\n